; A `.cbor` payload applied INLINE to a target that is itself a `.cbor` payload
; (`bytes .cbor (bytes .cbor uint)`), in MEMBER position. Supported: both depths live in ONE type
; expression and therefore in one `RustType`'s encoding chain, and every name the payload machinery
; mints is per OWNING VARIABLE — the staging buffer (`<var>_inner_se`), the byte vector, the reader
; over it, the staging local, and the `--preserve-encodings` sidecar member — so the levels are
; separated by the depth suffix each of those names carries (`cbor_bytes_infix` and its siblings).
; Level 1 keeps the historical spelling, which is what keeps every single-payload output unmoved.
;
; Member position is deliberate: the composition, not the position, is what was ever at issue, and
; the rule-BODY spelling (`b = bytes .cbor (bytes .cbor uint)`) reaches the other parse seam — the
; one that force-wraps. tests/robustness/cbor_payload_same_chain_alias.cddl is the NAMED-payload
; sibling, where the nesting crosses a real type boundary instead.
;
; This catalog row asserts generation only. The executed byte evidence is
; `cbor_payload_inline_nesting` in tests/core/tests.rs (hand-derived RFC 8949 oracle, triple
; nesting, a value-less payload, both leftover-byte refusals) and `cbor_inline_nested_payloads` in
; tests/preserve-encodings/tests.rs (each level's byte-string encoding driven independently).
foo = [b: bytes .cbor (bytes .cbor uint)]
